  "Number": 4,
  "Question": "If the side of one square is the diagonal of a second square, what is the ratio of the area of the first square to the area of the second?",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(2\\)",
    "B": "\\(\\sqrt2\\)",
    "C": "\\(\\frac12\\)",
    "D": "\\(2\\sqrt2\\)",
    "E": "\\(4\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "A",
  "Solution": "A square's area is its side length squared, so we only need the square of the second square's diagonal. Let its side be \\(s>0\\) and its diagonal be \\(d\\). The diagonal is the hypotenuse of a right triangle whose legs both have length \\(s\\). By the Pythagorean theorem,\n\\[\nd^2=s^2+s^2=2s^2.\n\\]\nThe first square has side \\(d\\), so its area is \\(d^2\\); the second has area \\(s^2\\). Since \\(s^2>0\\), their ratio is\n\\[\n\\frac{d^2}{s^2}=\\boxed{2},\n\\]\nchoice A.",
